WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
Good morning, John, I'm going to Comic-Con, 
but we need to talk

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.400
and maybe we should've been talking about this 
for a while, and I know it's not super fun

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.020
but the internet has become like 
a less...a less kind place.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.760
It's unpleasant to admit, as 
a person who really loves the internet,

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.020
but it would appear that we've entered
 into a bit of an age of outrage.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.700
This is not a problem with humans,
or with the internet;

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.580
it's a problem of humans mixed with the internet.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.800
I think that we'd all kind of like to talk
 as much as we're listened to.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.380
Which works if it's a two person conversation,

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.100
like, one person talks, the other person talks

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.700
50/50 split is a pretty good thing.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.500
And I think we've all felt this with like a six or eight person conversation,

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.560
that it can get a little frustrating 
when you can't get a word in edgewise

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.700
cause you're just not the loudest or 
the most eloquent person in the room.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.580
In a billion-person conversation

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:45.760
(and yeah, I'm in San Diego now
 'cause I had to get on my plane)

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.560
it's more like just a deafening roar
accompanied by feelings of powerlessness.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.180
I think we are in the first age of the
 social internet and the job of that age

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.280
is to suck us in and capture our eyeballs

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.320
and keep them as close to those revenue-generating advertisements as possible.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.080
Research has shown that even over cute things or inspirational things or amazing things,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.840
angering things, things that outrage us; that's what 
our brains, on average, are most prone to sharing.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.720
The problem is, whether it makes us angry 
definitely matters to whether we share it;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:21.020
whether it's true or not, whether it's productive or not...not really a factor.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.720
Maybe we need to enter a new stage of the social internet:

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:29.140
One where we choose and we create places on the internet that aren't just addictive or distracting

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:33.760
but instead bring personal and societal satisfaction.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.200
Throughout our lives we know this:
we have to work to keep ourselves healthy

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.940
and to keep our relationships healthy
and to keep our communities healthy

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.440
and the internet is a huge part of our communities now.
And a lot of it is unhealthy.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.320
These things don't take care of themselves,
and we had this idea early on that they would,

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:56.480
but the land in my city is not responsible for the wellbeing of the community that exists on top of it.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.300
Keeping that community healthy is the job of the government and the citizens,

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:03.440
and so keeping internet communities healthy is the job of the leaders of those communities,

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.280
whether they're the owners or the people 
working for those communities,

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.940
or the leaders in those communities,
and also the citizens of those communities.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:14.280
Without conscious leadership, these communities 
decay, and they destroy themselves,

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:20.100
and they leach out into the real world where they harm actual individuals humans and our culture.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.420
I've linked to a couple of videos in the description that helped me think about this stuff well

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.360
if you wanna watch some other perspectives on it.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.460
I've been so inspired by seeing the positive effects of the "plussing" that we've been doing.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:36.260
For me the best comments are content themselves;
they add instead of just react.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.760
So I've been loving it but there are some people
who have pointed out problems with plussing,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.820
particularly that it makes discussion 
more difficult when it's in a sea of pluses.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.980
For those people, a nerdfighter named Melvin 
created a Chrome extension that will

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.740
remove the "+" comments from YouTube 
comments. So that's...thank you Melvin!

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.500
I've always been proud of this community 
and I have always known that it's

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.840
composed almost entirely of kind, thoughtful people.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:02.060
I know that because of the comment section, I know that because I've met so many of you in the real world.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.900
I know it because at Vidcon this year
we had volunteers going around

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.080
and giving these wristbands to anyone who would

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.140
donate to help the families and the victims of the Pulse nightclub shooting.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.860
And then Vidcon and Universal Talent Agency and YouTube all matched those donations

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.520
until we raised almost $40,000.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.860
And we actually have some left over, 
so if you want to continue supporting that effort

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.440
you can go to dftba.com, where they're for sale for $5.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.200
But in the end, in a world of infinite content, 
when "shareability" is king,

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.080
sometimes kindness and empathy
don't play particularly well,

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.260
and so we start to forget they exist.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.620
But I do think that we're gonna start heading into 
a new age of the social internet

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.660
And it's not gonna happen immediately or anything,

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.720
where platforms and the users of those platforms

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.180
work together to actually promote good community,

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:45.900
and kindness, and empathy,

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.420
and that's certainly happening 
in certain corners of the internet,

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.920
and I'm very proud that it's happening right here.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.920
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

